<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rosa Parks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1913">1913</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rosa Louise McCauley Parks</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rosa Parks was an American civil rights activist, best known for refusing on 1 December 1955 to give up her seat on a bus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-alabama">Montgomery, Alabama</a>, to a white passenger, in defiance of Jim Crow segregation laws. A seamstress and, from 1943, secretary of the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a> chapter, she was arrested and fined, and the bus boycott that followed lasted 381 days, until bus segregation was ruled unconstitutional in Browder v. Gayle. She moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> in 1957 and is sometimes called the &quot;mother of the civil rights movement&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosa-parks.jpg" alt="Rosa Parks" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Parks,_November_1956_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rosa Louise McCauley was born on 4 February 1913 in Tuskegee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, the daughter of Leona, a teacher, and James McCauley, a carpenter and mason. As a baby she moved with her mother to Pine Level, Alabama, where she attended the African Methodist Episcopal church, to which she belonged all her life. She completed high school in 1933 with the encouragement of her husband, Raymond Parks, at a time when only 7 per cent of Black people in Alabama held a diploma. In 1941 she began work at Maxwell Air Force Base, which was fully integrated, and said it opened her eyes to an alternative to Jim Crow; on her way home she still had to use segregated buses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Parks joined the Montgomery chapter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a> in 1943 and served as its secretary. After three attempts between 1943 and 1945 she registered to vote despite measures to disenfranchise Black citizens, and she investigated cases of racial and sexual violence, among them those of Recy Taylor and Jeremiah Reeves. Several women had been arrested before her for keeping their seats on Montgomery buses, including Claudette Colvin, aged fifteen, in March 1955, but activists judged Colvin unsuitable as a test case. After Parks's arrest, E. D. Nixon and Clifford Durr favoured her for her standing in the community and her firm quiet spirit.</p><p>She agreed after consulting her family, and the lawyer Fred Gray took her case. The Women's Political Council, led by Jo Ann Robinson, had 35,000 leaflets printed announcing a one-day boycott for 5 December 1955, the day of her trial. After she was found guilty the boycott was extended, and the Montgomery Improvement Association ran a network of transport to sustain it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Parks and her husband lost their jobs and fell into hardship, and in 1957 they moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a>. She took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a> in August 1963, and later supported the Free South Africa Movement. She died on 24 October 2005 aged 92. After a service in Montgomery she lay in state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-capitol">United States Capitol</a> rotunda, where 40,000 mourners came, and was buried in Detroit. She received the Spingarn Medal in 1979, the Presidential Medal of Freedom and a Congressional Gold Medal, and was the first Black American memorialised in the National Statuary Hall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rosa Parks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rosa Parks</b> was not the first to refuse: in March 1955, nine months before her arrest, the fifteen-year-old Claudette Colvin was arrested for keeping her seat on a Montgomery bus, and she was a plaintiff in the case that desegregated them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rosa Parks</b> was arrested in Montgomery, Alabama, for refusing to give her seat to a white passenger.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tuskegee, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.43,-85.71&amp;year=1972" aria-label="Tuskegee on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rosa Parks"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5310 4786h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5310 4786h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.43,-85.71&amp;year=1972">What was here in 1972? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
